{:edb/db-before-t 1398 :edb/db-after-t 1399 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534711 50 #inst "2026-10-08T04:48:29.332Z" 13194139534711 true] [17592186078386 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304873 13194139534711 true] [17592186078386 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304911 13194139534711 true] [17592186078435 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304880 13194139534711 true] [17592186078435 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304894 13194139534711 true] [17592186078435 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304897 13194139534711 true] [17592186078435 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304901 13194139534711 true] [17592186078443 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304866 13194139534711 true] [17592186078443 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304887 13194139534711 true] [17592186078443 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304904 13194139534711 true] [17592186078443 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304908 13194139534711 true] [17592186079020 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534711 true] [17592186079020 1100 "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x},\\,y(1)=2.\n$$\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=1$ to approximate $y(1.5).$" 13194139534711 true] [17592186079020 1100 "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$y^{′} = \\frac{1}{x}, y(1) = 2$.\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x = 1$ to approximate $y(1.5)$." 13194139534711 false] [17592186079020 1145 "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(1)+{y}^{′}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(1)=2.$ We also know that ${y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{x},$ so\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(1)$ and ${y}^{′}(1)$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(1)+{y}^{′}(1)(x-1) \\\\  & =2+(1)(x-1) \\\\  & =x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y(1.5)$ using $L(1.5).$ We get\n\n$$\ny(1.5)≈L(1.5)=1.5+1=2.5.\n$$" 13194139534711 true] [17592186079128 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534711 true] [17592186079128 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3x-4y,\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $$ | $$ | $\\,?\\,$ |" 13194139534711 true] [17592186079128 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$y^{′} = 3x - 4y, y(1) = 2$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx = \\frac{1}{2}$?\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ |  |  | $$? |" 13194139534711 false] [17592186079128 1145 "First, because the initial condition is $y(1)=2,$ we fill $y=2$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3x-4y \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $$ |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ |" 13194139534711 true] [17592186079131 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534711 true] [17592186079131 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=5x+6y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with one step to approximate $y(1).$" 13194139534711 true] [17592186079131 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$y^{′} = 5x + 6y, y(0) = 1$\n\nUse Euler's method with one step to approximate $y(1)$." 13194139534711 false] [17592186079131 1145 "Since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of \n\n$$\nΔx=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =5x+6y \\\\  & =5\\cdot (0)+6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $6$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(6)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $6$ | $6$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+6 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $6$ | $6$ |\n| $1$ | $7$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈7.$" 13194139534711 true] [17592186304866 1017 #edb/ref 17592186304867 13194139534711 true] [17592186304866 1089 "q-116773" 13194139534711 true] [17592186304866 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534711 true] [17592186304866 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2xy,\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the cell indicated in the table below if the step size is $Δx=1?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ |  | $$ | $$ |\n| $2$ | $\\,?\\,$ | $$ | $$ |" 13194139534711 true] [17592186304866 1113 #uuid "d3a86cf7-ac7c-5cf3-be46-a948a6ac4cec" 13194139534711 true] [17592186304866 1145 "First, because the initial condition is $y(1)=1,$ we fill $y=1$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2xy \\\\  & =2\\cdot (1)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈3.$" 13194139534711 true] [17592186304867 1000 #edb/ref 17592186304868 13194139534711 true] [17592186304867 1000 #edb/ref 17592186304869 13194139534711 true] [17592186304867 1000 #edb/ref 17592186304870 13194139534711 true] [17592186304867 1000 #edb/ref 17592186304871 13194139534711 true] [17592186304867 1000 #edb/ref 17592186304872 13194139534711 true] [17592186304867 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534711 true] [17592186304867 1041 "selection" 13194139534711 true] [17592186304867 1104 #edb/ref 17592186304868 13194139534711 true] [17592186304867 1110 #uuid "c9c5da0a-013d-5da6-8eaa-