453 13194139534711 true] [17592186304907 1111 #uuid "d67ca3f0-5dcd-50a1-8ee0-20e5f8ffa181" 13194139534711 true] [17592186304908 1017 #edb/ref 17592186304909 13194139534711 true] [17592186304908 1089 "q-353325" 13194139534711 true] [17592186304908 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534711 true] [17592186304908 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x(2y-1),\\,y(\\frac{1}{2})=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with one step to approximate $y(1).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$y(1)≈$  \n{{field-1}}" 13194139534711 true] [17592186304908 1113 #uuid "4eddbf6a-7384-5c89-ae2f-33e9958b3093" 13194139534711 true] [17592186304908 1145 "Since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of \n\n$$\nΔx=1-\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x(2y-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 1-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈\\frac{5}{4}.$" 13194139534711 true] [17592186304909 1000 #edb/ref 17592186304910 13194139534711 true] [17592186304909 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534711 true] [17592186304909 1041 "field-1" 13194139534711 true] [17592186304909 1104 #edb/ref 17592186304910 13194139534711 true] [17592186304909 1110 #uuid "3d685e99-d281-5d9e-959c-31ba547aef9b" 13194139534711 true] [17592186304910 1034 "\\frac{5}{4}" 13194139534711 true] [17592186304910 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534711 true] [17592186304910 1111 #uuid "8081d013-8366-5453-852a-caae174c94e3" 13194139534711 true] [17592186304911 1017 #edb/ref 17592186304912 13194139534711 true] [17592186304911 1089 "q-83382" 13194139534711 true] [17592186304911 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534711 true] [17592186304911 1100 "Consider the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,y(3)=e.\n$$\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=3$ to approximate $y(4).$" 13194139534711 true] [17592186304911 1113 #uuid "0c0185af-134b-51d7-bfef-56a8c9ab82dd" 13194139534711 true] [17592186304911 1145 "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(3)+{y}^{′}(3)(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(3)=e.$ We also know that ${y}^{′}(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,$ so\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(3)=3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(3)$ and ${y}^{′}(3)$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(3)+{y}^{′}(3)(x-3) \\\\  & =e+(3)(x-3) \\\\  & =3x+e-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y(4)$ using $L(4).$ We get\n\n$$\ny(4)≈L(4)=3(4)+e-9=e+3\n$$" 13194139534711 true] [17592186304912 1000 #edb/ref 17592186304913 13194139534711 true] [17592186